Findings  x 2 errors | ! 6 warnings | 0 info

  1  x B is declared in negative/.env.example but missing from negative/.env
       error | EG001 missing-variable | negative/.env

     Why      Anything that reads B in the default environment gets no value,
              which usually fails only at runtime or silently falls back to a
              default.
     Fix      1. Add `B=` to negative/.env (it is declared in
                 negative/.env.example:1) and set its value.
              2. If it is not needed there, mark it optional in the template
                 (`B= # optional`) or put `# envguard-ignore EG001` on the line
                 above it in negative/.env.example.
     Related  negative/.env.example:1:1

  2  x TEMPLATE_VAR is declared in positive/.env.example but missing from
       positive/.env
       error | EG001 missing-variable | positive/.env

     Why      Anything that reads TEMPLATE_VAR in the default environment gets
              no value, which usually fails only at runtime or silently falls
              back to a default.
     Fix      1. Add `TEMPLATE_VAR=` to positive/.env (it is declared in
                 positive/.env.example:1) and set its value.
              2. If it is not needed there, mark it optional in the template
                 (`TEMPLATE_VAR= # optional`) or put `# envguard-ignore EG001`
                 on the line above it in positive/.env.example.
     Related  positive/.env.example:1:1

  3  ! A is set in negative/.env but not documented in any template
       warning | EG002 undocumented-variable | negative/.env:1:1

     Why      Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from
              the templates; without an entry, A will be missing wherever this
              file is not copied.
     Fix      Add `A=` (with an empty or placeholder value, never the real one)
              to negative/.env.example, or remove it from negative/.env if
              nothing uses it.

  4  ! DEFINED_VAR is set in positive/.env but not documented in any template
       warning | EG002 undocumented-variable | positive/.env:1:1

     Why      Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from
              the templates; without an entry, DEFINED_VAR will be missing
              wherever this file is not copied.
     Fix      Add `DEFINED_VAR=` (with an empty or placeholder value, never the
              real one) to positive/.env.example, or remove it from
              positive/.env if nothing uses it.

  5  ! positive/deploy/compose.prod.yaml interpolates ${PROD_ONLY}, but there is
       no positive/deploy/.env next to it and it is not declared in
       positive/.env.example
       warning | EG011 compose-undefined-variable | positive/deploy/compose.prod.yaml:3:19

     Why      Compose substitutes an empty string for an unset PROD_ONLY and
              only prints a warning, so the container starts with a broken
              value.
     Fix      Create positive/deploy/.env with `PROD_ONLY=<value>` (Compose
              reads it automatically), declare `PROD_ONLY=` in a template so
              others know to set it, or give it a default:
              `${PROD_ONLY:-value}`.

  6  ! positive/docker-compose.yml interpolates ${DB_PASSWORD}, which is not
       defined in positive/.env (the file Compose reads) or any template
       warning | EG011 compose-undefined-variable | positive/docker-compose.yml:5:39

     Why      Compose substitutes an empty string for an unset DB_PASSWORD and
              only prints a warning, so the container starts with a broken
              value.
     Fix      Add `DB_PASSWORD=` with a value to positive/.env and document it
              in a template, or give it a default in the Compose file:
              `${DB_PASSWORD:-value}`.
     Related  positive/docker-compose.yml:10:16

  7  ! positive/docker-compose.yml interpolates ${REQUIRED_TOKEN}, which is not
       defined in positive/.env (the file Compose reads) or any template
       warning | EG011 compose-undefined-variable | positive/docker-compose.yml:9:16

     Why      The reference uses ${REQUIRED_TOKEN:?...}, so `docker compose up`
              aborts when it is unset.
     Fix      Add `REQUIRED_TOKEN=` with a value to positive/.env and document
              it in a template, or give it a default in the Compose file:
              `${REQUIRED_TOKEN:-value}`.

  8  ! service api: env_file positive/missing.env does not exist
       warning | EG011 compose-undefined-variable | positive/docker-compose.yml:12:9

     Why      `docker compose up` fails for this service when a required
              env_file is missing.
     Fix      Create positive/missing.env (for example by copying its template),
              or make it optional with the long syntax:
                env_file:
                  - path: ./missing.env
                    required: false

